# aql — working todo

Scratch list. The structured version is ROADMAP.md; this is the running one.

## direction (see ARCHITECTURE.md)
- Aql = one hub that owns everything physical. Seven device kinds: camera, lighting,
  robot, climate, energy, sensor, access. Access is the most complete and the only one
  that does NOT go through the device engine — it is still a parallel stack.
- Chat is an input surface across the hub, not the product.
- Shipped: the hub (open path, console, audit, SQLite), the controller agent
  (pinned-key pairing, fail-closed verification, offline LAN grants), signed commands,
  proto/'s conformance vectors, the e2e harnesses, the device engine + four drivers,
  the automations runtime, energy metering, and the console screens over all of it.

## naming, settled
- The hub is a **hub**. Never "the gateway" in prose — in the KOTVA family that word is
  the legacy-rail coordinator role, which is Ephor's job. The directory is `hub/`.
- Still spelled the old way, on purpose, as compat surface: `LINTEL_*` env vars (read
  behind `lookupEnv`, with a startup warning), `lintel.db`, `_lintel._tcp`,
  `lintel.offline-access` (an IndexedDB rename orphans rather than migrates), and
  proto/vectors/. Guarded by src/lib/__tests__/naming.test.ts.

## next up
- [ ] BLE radio validation on real Linux/BlueZ hardware — framing and session are tested,
      the radio has never run. `-tags ble`; every other platform returns ErrUnsupported
      from `bleperiph/start_unsupported.go`.
- [ ] GPIO relay validation on real hardware — the driver exists behind `-tags gpio` and
      has never driven a chip, a relay board or a gate. Fail-safe spec: normally-open,
      line drops on exit/panic.
- [ ] Present an offline grant from a phone. Requesting and storing works and is proven
      end to end (e2e-browser/safety-copy.spec.ts); *presenting* talks straight to the
      controller over LAN or BLE, which a browser tab cannot do.
- [ ] Bring access onto the internal device model, so it is one kind among seven rather
      than a parallel stack.
- [ ] A robot driver — the one kind of the seven with no path at all.

## blocked on something outside this repo
- [ ] Matter — needs a certified device and a stack.
- [ ] Modbus RTU (serial). TCP ships; a TCP-to-RTU bridge covers the common case.
- [ ] The camera pipeline: H.264 depacketization and an fMP4 writer. The ONVIF driver
      discovers cameras and probes streams with RTSP and has never received a frame.
      `docs/CAMERA-RETENTION.md` is the design that has to exist first — it is design
      only, and steps 3–5 in it (retention worker, `camera:view` permission, viewer) do
      NOT need hardware.
- [ ] Ephor chat-rail migration steps 1–7 (docs/EPHOR-CHAT-SEAM.md) — needs a kotva retag
      and a crate that does not exist yet.
- [ ] COORD-2 — filed to ephor/COORDINATION.md, awaiting a spec ruling. `LockIn` has two
      states and cannot express mode-dependent lock-in. Pinned by a test that fails when
      the answer changes.

## chat rail → Ephor (in progress)
- [ ] the WhatsApp / Slack / Telegram adapters move OUT of Aql and into Ephor
      (github.com/vul-os/ephor). Target shape: Ephor terminates the rail and hands the hub
      an authorised command; the hub checks rules, signs, actuates. Swappable — run one or
      point at one.
- [ ] `hub/internal/channels/` is transitional until that lands. Don't document it as
      the supported path; don't rip it out either — it's what works today.
- [ ] `scripts/feature-claims.manifest.mjs` still has `whatsapp-channel`, `slack-channel`,
      `telegram-channel`, `slack-socket-mode` as shipped Aql claims. The code IS still
      there, so those entries are truthful today — revisit when the move lands rather
      than weakening them now.
- [x] Telegram long-polling (`getUpdates`) — shipped as an opt-in engine
      (`AQL_TELEGRAM_ENGINE=polling`). The poller had been complete and tested for a
      while with nothing constructing it; wiring is now covered by a reachability test.
- [ ] Discord — designed into the seam, no code yet. Same question: whose seam?
- [ ] extend the intent vocabulary past `open`/`close` so chat reaches the other kinds
- [ ] DMTAP channel stays a scaffold with a fail-closed transport until there is a real
      wire format. Do not present it as a channel.

## console ↔ hub drift (tracked by src/lib/__tests__/routeParity.test.ts)
CLOSED. routeParity's KNOWN_UNAVAILABLE list is empty — every route api.ts calls, the
hub serves. Adding an entry back is a real decision, not bookkeeping.
- [ ] Phone linking is the one that was NOT a missing route: the hub has owned
      phone→member mapping since 0002 and requires verified_at to grant access, but
      nothing has ever verified a number, and both obvious shortcuts let an attacker
      squat a neighbour's number. Design + the squatting hazard: docs/PHONE-LINKING.md.
      Migration 0018 is unclaimed and reserved for it.
- Not doing: Google OAuth, and any email-based auth. The hub is meant to work off nodes;
  recovery is username-based through a delivery seam, not an email dependency.

## api & integrations
- [ ] a token-scoped `GET /v1/events` feed (today the audit log is instance-admin only)
- [ ] revoke-a-controller endpoint (no DELETE on /v1/devices yet)
- [ ] API docs with python/go/curl examples

## conformance
- [x] store/tenants.go's six superseded methods are gone; the tenancy-scoping test now
      drives the accessors production uses.
- [ ] ~32 uncalled exported symbols remain. Two of the six with NO caller at all
      (not even a test) turned out to matter: NewTelegramPoller was an entire unwired
      feature, and jcs.CanonicalizeJSON was an unrun entry point in a safety-critical
      package. The four left are UnmarshalJSON (encoding/json calls it) and three
      energy functional options. RESOLVED: all three were seams for behaviour nothing
      tested (the poller's clock on untimed readings, the per-device read timeout, the
      rollup budget), each confirmed by tampering first. They earn their place; they now
      have callers. UnmarshalJSON is called by encoding/json and is fine.
- LESSON: "no caller" is a question, not a verdict. Of the six symbols with no caller at
      all, one was an unwired feature, one an unrun entry point in a safety-critical
      package, three were untested seams, and one was a false positive. Delete last. Most are legitimate —
      test helpers, functional options, build-tagged constructors, encoding/json
      interface methods — but the sweep is worth re-running after any big change. The
      first version of it reported ZERO because a function's own doc comment counts as
      a use; strip comments before counting.

## security / ops
- [ ] grant revocation beyond "wait for the TTL" (accepted v0 non-goal today)
- [ ] OS-keychain credential vault for device/service secrets — ROADMAP Phase 2. Today
      there is no keychain/keyring code at all, and the hub's signing key + JWT secret sit
      unencrypted in the data directory
- [ ] controller position/tamper sensors return static values — wire real ones
- [ ] pen test the pairing + signed-command path against the proto vectors
- [ ] publish the aql-hub image automatically (docker.yml is workflow_dispatch only)

## hygiene
- [ ] `src-tauri`'s `system_pulse` IPC command is never called by the frontend. It is
      also cited in docs as the reason the Rust core was not chosen for the device
      engine, so deleting it costs that example — decide which.
- [ ] test the WhatsApp flow end to end at a real gate: multi-access-point picker,
      location-first flow, "hi" fallback lists linked locations or says not linked

## do not resurrect (removed by decision)
- billing / wallet / subscriptions / invoices / VAT / referrals / payouts / pricing tiers
  — Aql has NO billing system. Operators solve money outside the system; see
  ARCHITECTURE.md §4 "Money is out of scope".
- the Cloudflare Workers + Postgres backend in `backend/`. Deleted. The Go hub is the
  only server, and nothing may cite `backend/src/...` as a spec again — guarded by
  naming.test.ts.
- the SvelteKit shell, and the demo dataset it brought with it. `src/lib/demoData.ts` is
  deleted; every device row comes from the engine.
- renaming `LINTEL_*`, `lintel.db`, `_lintel._tcp` or the offline vault's IndexedDB name.
  Deployment and wire contract for hardware already in the field.
